1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,490
Why do so many people hate Windows?

2
00:00:03,419 --> 00:00:06,249
Windows runs on more
than a billion computers.

3
00:00:07,279 --> 00:00:09,139
For most of those people it is simply the

4
00:00:09,219 --> 00:00:11,329
thing that starts when
they press the button.

5
00:00:12,093 --> 00:00:16,543
They open a browser. Watch YouTube.
Write a document. Play a game.

6
00:00:17,311 --> 00:00:20,361
And they never think about
what Windows is doing inside.

7
00:00:20,791 --> 00:00:23,461
Or about the fact that
anything else exists.

8
00:00:24,449 --> 00:00:28,159
But some people can talk for an
hour about why Windows is terrible.

9
00:00:28,939 --> 00:00:32,299
Some of them run Linux.
Some of them have a Mac.

10
00:00:32,379 --> 00:00:34,149
Some simply don't like Microsoft.

11
00:00:35,181 --> 00:00:37,031
So what exactly bothers them?

12
00:00:37,701 --> 00:00:39,911
And why might none of it
make any sense to you?

13
00:00:42,421 --> 00:00:44,431
Let's start with something concrete.

14
00:00:45,440 --> 00:00:49,590
Some programs written in the
1990s still run on Windows today.

15
00:00:50,500 --> 00:00:54,810
Thirty years later, on hardware that
didn't exist when they were written.

16
00:00:55,543 --> 00:00:57,973
Almost nothing else you own can say that.

17
00:00:58,754 --> 00:01:00,604
And here is the strange part.

18
00:01:00,934 --> 00:01:04,604
That is also one of the reasons
people call Windows a mess.

19
00:01:05,444 --> 00:01:07,204
The thing they complain
about and the thing

20
00:01:07,284 --> 00:01:09,494
that keeps it working are the same thing.

21
00:01:12,464 --> 00:01:14,474
But first, let's start with you.

22
00:01:15,269 --> 00:01:20,169
Imagine you buy a washing machine. You
plug it in. You select a programme.

23
00:01:20,249 --> 00:01:21,779
It washes your clothes.

24
00:01:22,729 --> 00:01:24,599
You don't want to read its source code.

25
00:01:25,249 --> 00:01:29,219
You don't want to write your own
spin cycle. You want clean clothes.

26
00:01:30,168 --> 00:01:35,848
And that is how most people see their
computer. The computer works. Done.

27
00:01:35,928 --> 00:01:37,638
And there is nothing wrong with that.

28
00:01:39,788 --> 00:01:41,818
But some people want something more.

29
00:01:42,628 --> 00:01:46,098
And to see why, we have to look
at how Windows was actually built.

30
00:01:46,926 --> 00:01:50,136
Today's Windows was never
designed as one system.

31
00:01:51,126 --> 00:01:53,876
It is the result of more
than forty years of building.

32
00:01:54,683 --> 00:01:58,443
It started as a graphical
shell running on top of MS-DOS.

33
00:01:59,083 --> 00:02:01,933
Almost everything since
then has been added on top.

34
00:02:02,695 --> 00:02:04,715
And Microsoft had one enormous constraint.

35
00:02:04,795 --> 00:02:06,705
Compatibility.

36
00:02:07,828 --> 00:02:11,738
Imagine a building that has been
repaired and extended for thirty years.

37
00:02:12,511 --> 00:02:14,561
The original wiring is outdated.

38
00:02:15,551 --> 00:02:18,481
Some pipes run in
completely illogical places.

39
00:02:19,311 --> 00:02:21,101
Today you would design it differently.

40
00:02:21,997 --> 00:02:23,567
But people still live there.

41
00:02:24,257 --> 00:02:26,027
Businesses still operate there.

42
00:02:26,337 --> 00:02:28,547
So you can't demolish it and start again.

43
00:02:29,287 --> 00:02:31,197
You add a floor. You fit a lift.

44
00:02:31,627 --> 00:02:34,897
And you keep the old pipes,
because nobody may touch them.

45
00:02:35,593 --> 00:02:37,603
Windows can be a bit like that.

46
00:02:38,419 --> 00:02:41,399
A program written twenty years
ago may still expect a particular

47
00:02:41,479 --> 00:02:46,149
interface, a particular behaviour,
a particular file format.

48
00:02:46,942 --> 00:02:49,502
And Microsoft can't simply
delete the old parts.

49
00:02:49,582 --> 00:02:53,552
Because somewhere in the world
a company still depends on them.

50
00:02:54,206 --> 00:02:55,406
That is the paradox.

51
00:02:56,366 --> 00:02:59,576
Backward compatibility is one
of Windows' greatest strengths.

52
00:03:00,086 --> 00:03:03,796
And one of the reasons it feels
like a technological patchwork.

53
00:03:06,666 --> 00:03:09,316
And some versions of
Windows really didn't help.

54
00:03:10,069 --> 00:03:12,149
Windows 95. Windows 98.

55
00:03:12,229 --> 00:03:14,279
Windows ME.

56
00:03:15,159 --> 00:03:18,429
Drivers. Conflicts. Crashes.
Restarts. Blue screens.

57
00:03:19,265 --> 00:03:23,875
Installing a new piece of hardware could
feel like a small engineering project.

58
00:03:24,588 --> 00:03:29,898
And Windows earned a reputation.
Windows is buggy. Windows crashes.

59
00:03:30,088 --> 00:03:31,878
Windows needs reinstalling.

60
00:03:32,624 --> 00:03:37,774
But underneath, Microsoft had a
second, sturdier line. Windows NT.

61
00:03:38,484 --> 00:03:43,614
And in 2001, Windows XP finally
brought it to ordinary home computers.

62
00:03:44,564 --> 00:03:47,224
Windows became a far more stable system.

63
00:03:47,304 --> 00:03:49,534
But the reputation stayed.

64
00:03:50,337 --> 00:03:53,327
If a restaurant serves you
one genuinely terrible meal,

65
00:03:53,637 --> 00:03:56,607
you can come back ten years
later and find it excellent.

66
00:03:57,473 --> 00:04:01,483
And you will still think: I once had
the worst goulash of my life in here.

67
00:04:04,263 --> 00:04:06,313
And then there is Microsoft itself.

68
00:04:07,260 --> 00:04:11,230
Windows became the dominant operating
system for personal computers.

69
00:04:12,260 --> 00:04:13,910
And with dominance comes power.

70
00:04:14,782 --> 00:04:18,552
Microsoft ended up in a long series
of fights with its competitors.

71
00:04:19,822 --> 00:04:24,292
Most famously the antitrust case
around Internet Explorer and Netscape.

72
00:04:24,992 --> 00:04:27,862
So there is a second reason
people dislike Windows.

73
00:04:28,172 --> 00:04:30,632
Not because it crashed on them.

74
00:04:30,712 --> 00:04:34,422
But because of how the company
behaved when it was winning.

75
00:04:35,161 --> 00:04:38,771
So you can dislike Windows without
having any problem with its technology.

76
00:04:42,021 --> 00:04:44,091
But now we get to something deeper.

77
00:04:44,955 --> 00:04:47,245
Imagine you buy a car. It is yours.

78
00:04:48,187 --> 00:04:52,777
And then the manufacturer says: some of
its functions can be changed by an update.

79
00:04:53,744 --> 00:04:56,394
Some parts have to be paired
with the car's electronics.

80
00:04:57,424 --> 00:05:01,854
And some things simply aren't possible,
because we decided not to allow them.

81
00:05:02,555 --> 00:05:04,185
Most people won't care.

82
00:05:04,735 --> 00:05:06,465
They will never open the bonnet.

83
00:05:07,239 --> 00:05:09,269
But some people will say: wait.

84
00:05:09,379 --> 00:05:10,789
I bought this.

85
00:05:11,319 --> 00:05:13,909
Why are you telling me what
I am allowed to do with it?

86
00:05:14,614 --> 00:05:16,734
And that feeling gets sharper
the day the manufacturer

87
00:05:16,814 --> 00:05:19,904
announces it is dropping
a feature you were using.

88
00:05:20,619 --> 00:05:23,389
You can complain. You
can buy a different car.

89
00:05:24,239 --> 00:05:27,229
But you cannot take their software
and keep the feature working.

90
00:05:28,035 --> 00:05:30,905
And this is where the philosophy
of free software begins.

91
00:05:33,795 --> 00:05:37,405
Windows is proprietary
software. Closed source.

92
00:05:38,266 --> 00:05:40,456
Microsoft gives you the finished program.

93
00:05:41,246 --> 00:05:43,236
You don't get its complete source code.

94
00:05:44,039 --> 00:05:47,969
It is a car where the manufacturer
says: this is how we built it.

95
00:05:48,599 --> 00:05:50,309
Trust us that it works properly.

96
00:05:51,198 --> 00:05:53,608
And most people will say: fine.

97
00:05:53,778 --> 00:05:57,748
Just as most people never open
up their car's control unit.

98
00:05:58,485 --> 00:06:01,235
But some people will say:
I would like to look.

99
00:06:02,059 --> 00:06:07,579
With open source you can read the
code. Study it. Change it. Fix it.

100
00:06:07,659 --> 00:06:10,969
And if the licence allows
it, build your own version.

101
00:06:11,761 --> 00:06:15,331
Closed source doesn't mean that
software cannot be checked at all.

102
00:06:16,017 --> 00:06:18,217
You can watch how a program behaves.

103
00:06:18,297 --> 00:06:20,727
You can watch its network traffic.

104
00:06:20,917 --> 00:06:25,467
You can take the binary apart, all the
way down to processor instructions.

105
00:06:26,719 --> 00:06:30,909
But that is dismantling the finished car
instead of reading the service manual.

106
00:06:31,786 --> 00:06:33,416
In theory, you can do it.

107
00:06:34,086 --> 00:06:35,796
In practice, it is much harder.

108
00:06:36,641 --> 00:06:38,841
So closed software asks you for a certain

109
00:06:38,921 --> 00:06:41,711
amount of trust in the
company that made it.

110
00:06:42,391 --> 00:06:47,081
And that, far more than any crash, is what
some people are actually objecting to.

111
00:06:47,777 --> 00:06:50,327
Because the argument was
never really about bugs.

112
00:06:51,217 --> 00:06:52,607
It was about who decides.

113
00:06:55,967 --> 00:06:57,587
By the way, if you'd like to see where the

114
00:06:57,667 --> 00:07:00,517
next question takes us,
consider subscribing.

115
00:07:01,407 --> 00:07:02,877
It really helps the channel.

116
00:07:04,060 --> 00:07:07,090
Which is why Linux isn't
simply Windows for free.

117
00:07:07,880 --> 00:07:09,530
It is also a different philosophy.

118
00:07:10,558 --> 00:07:13,608
Windows tells you: this
is how we designed it.

119
00:07:14,281 --> 00:07:17,211
Linux tends to say: here is the toolbox.

120
00:07:18,046 --> 00:07:22,636
You choose a distribution. A
desktop environment. Your programs.

121
00:07:22,866 --> 00:07:25,396
And if you want to, the system itself.

122
00:07:26,167 --> 00:07:27,367
But you don't have to.

123
00:07:27,907 --> 00:07:32,817
Install a mainstream distribution today
and you may never open a terminal at all.

124
00:07:33,561 --> 00:07:35,721
And if you are feeling
brave, you can still break

125
00:07:35,801 --> 00:07:38,471
something you didn't
understand five minutes ago...

126
00:07:39,261 --> 00:07:41,731
and then spend three
hours finding out why.

127
00:07:42,541 --> 00:07:44,021
That is Linux too.

128
00:07:44,221 --> 00:07:46,711
But it is an option,
not the price of entry.

129
00:07:47,695 --> 00:07:50,285
For some people that
freedom is the whole point.

130
00:07:51,015 --> 00:07:54,165
For everyone else it simply
sits there until they need it.

131
00:07:56,665 --> 00:07:58,635
And now an interesting paradox.

132
00:07:59,619 --> 00:08:02,549
If closed source is your
problem, you can move to Linux.

133
00:08:03,639 --> 00:08:07,209
But if convenience is what you
want, you can buy an Apple product.

134
00:08:08,123 --> 00:08:10,813
Apple builds the hardware
and the software together,

135
00:08:11,043 --> 00:08:13,713
so it has far more control
over the whole ecosystem.

136
00:08:14,704 --> 00:08:18,574
And that has real advantages.
Things work together.

137
00:08:19,224 --> 00:08:21,534
You don't have to think
about the technical details.

138
00:08:22,591 --> 00:08:25,181
And that is exactly what some people want.

139
00:08:25,851 --> 00:08:28,541
They don't want to be the
administrator of their computer.

140
00:08:29,431 --> 00:08:30,921
They want to be its user.

141
00:08:31,736 --> 00:08:34,326
To someone else the same
sentence is the problem.

142
00:08:35,196 --> 00:08:39,406
The more decisions the manufacturer
makes for you, the fewer are left to you.

143
00:08:40,090 --> 00:08:42,880
And there is one more thing
people hold against Apple.

144
00:08:43,730 --> 00:08:46,340
Every year, something
is revolutionary again.

145
00:08:47,121 --> 00:08:49,201
For some people that is part of the fun.

146
00:08:49,281 --> 00:08:52,871
For others it is exactly
why they can't stand it.

147
00:08:53,641 --> 00:08:55,871
They don't want a computer
to become a lifestyle.

148
00:08:59,081 --> 00:09:01,331
Windows says: you want compatibility?

149
00:09:02,541 --> 00:09:05,731
Here is a system that has to work
with an enormous number of things.

150
00:09:06,603 --> 00:09:10,773
Apple says: you want it all to
fit together? We will decide how.

151
00:09:11,949 --> 00:09:14,689
Linux says: you want control? Here you go.

152
00:09:14,769 --> 00:09:16,419
As much or as little as you like.

153
00:09:17,210 --> 00:09:19,860
And none of those is
automatically the right one.

154
00:09:22,790 --> 00:09:26,120
There is one more reason people
actively dislike Windows.

155
00:09:26,370 --> 00:09:29,820
And it has nothing to do with the 1990s.

156
00:09:30,419 --> 00:09:33,769
The feeling that the system is
constantly offering you something.

157
00:09:34,525 --> 00:09:39,305
Updates. OneDrive. A Microsoft
account. Edge. Notifications.

158
00:09:39,385 --> 00:09:41,595
Features you never asked for.

159
00:09:42,220 --> 00:09:44,450
And you might think: I
bought this computer.

160
00:09:45,300 --> 00:09:47,450
Why does it keep trying
to sell me something?

161
00:09:48,291 --> 00:09:53,721
There are obviously reasons behind those
decisions. Cloud services. Security.

162
00:09:54,691 --> 00:09:56,241
One ecosystem. Money.

163
00:09:56,967 --> 00:10:00,317
But from where the user is
standing, the result is very simple.

164
00:10:01,107 --> 00:10:02,717
Just let me use my computer.

165
00:10:05,817 --> 00:10:07,547
So who is actually right?

166
00:10:08,473 --> 00:10:12,533
Maybe everyone. Because people
arguing about operating systems

167
00:10:12,613 --> 00:10:15,203
are usually not answering
the same question.

168
00:10:16,081 --> 00:10:18,191
One of them says: I just want it to work.

169
00:10:18,961 --> 00:10:21,251
The other says: I want
to know how it works.

170
00:10:22,109 --> 00:10:24,419
One wants somebody else
to take care of it.

171
00:10:25,189 --> 00:10:27,879
The other wants nobody
telling them what they can do.

172
00:10:28,569 --> 00:10:30,239
Convenience, against control.

173
00:10:32,999 --> 00:10:34,669
And most people want neither.

174
00:10:35,359 --> 00:10:38,949
They want to turn the computer on,
open a browser, and have it work.

175
00:10:39,899 --> 00:10:42,409
Which is a perfectly
legitimate thing to want.

176
00:10:43,233 --> 00:10:47,403
So maybe the better question isn't
which operating system is best.

177
00:10:48,278 --> 00:10:51,328
Maybe it is: what do you
actually want from your computer?

178
00:10:52,299 --> 00:10:55,809
Convenience? Compatibility?
Freedom? Control?

179
00:10:56,749 --> 00:10:59,779
Or do you just want it to launch
your game when you turn it on?

180
00:11:00,515 --> 00:11:02,885
Because in the end, it is just a computer.

181
00:11:03,939 --> 00:11:05,709
Well... at least for most people.
